<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="pot-roll" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Potato Rolls (Grandma Joy)</title>

  <p>
    Ingredients
  </p>
  <tabular>
    <row>
      <cell><m>1 \frac{1}{2}</m> cups warm water (110-115 degrees)</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>1 package active dry yeast (<m>\frac{1}{4}</m> ounce)</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>\frac{2}{3}</m> cup sugar</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
    <cell>1 teaspoon salt</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>\frac{2}{3}</m> cup soft shortening</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>2 eggs</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>1 cup lukewarm mashed potatoes</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>7 to <m>7 \frac{1}{2}</m> cups flour</cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>

  <p>Instructions
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          In bowl, dissolve yeast in water.  (you can add about <m>\frac{1}{2}</m> teaspoon sugar to get it to grow better.) Stir in sugar, salt, shortening, eggs, and potatoes.  Mix in flour with hand until dough is easy to handle.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Turn onto lightly floured board.  Knead until smooth and elastic.   Place greased side up in a greased bowl.  Cover with damp cloth.  Place in refrigerator.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          About 2 hours before baking, shape dough into rolls.  (you can make 3 small balls to make clover-leafed rolls.)  Cover and let rise until double, <m>1 \frac{1}{2}</m> to 2 hours.  Bake at <m>400^\circ</m> for 11 minutes (13 minutes for pyrex cake pan).  Makes 4 dozen medium rolls.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <p>
    Notes:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</section>